-## Get Started
-
-### Desktop Node
-
-Requirements: Qt 6.4+ (Widgets, Network), CMake 3.16+, OpenSSL dev headers, Git, a C++17 compiler, and the `openssl` CLI at runtime for the LAN TLS certificate.
+**Code hosting that lives on the network, not in one provider's data center.**
+
+ForkMesh is a local-first developer platform for hosting, mirroring, browsing,
+and collaborating on Git repositories. Desktop nodes keep the Git data; a
+mainnode coordinates discovery, signed inboxes, encrypted rooms, and routing to
+an available host.
+
+ForkMesh is pre-1.0, but the core publish and collaboration loop is implemented
+and exercised by automated tests. This README describes the code that exists
+today. Current work is tracked in [`.forkmesh/issues/`](.forkmesh/issues/), and
+the protocol and operator documentation is served at
+[forkmesh.com/docs](https://forkmesh.com/docs).
+
+## What is built
+
+### Git hosting and preservation
+
+- Desktop nodes create and maintain local bare Git mirrors.
+- Published repositories can be browsed and cloned through stable web routes;
+  repository contents stream from a connected node rather than being stored in
+  the Worker.
+- If the source node disconnects, browsing and cloning can fail over to a
+  compatible mirror. Source-attested state pins guard the content served during
+  failover.
+- Repository owners can push over Git smart HTTP. `git-receive-pack` is streamed
+  to the owner's node and gated by a short-lived, owner-signed push token.
+- Public and private repository records are supported. The Qt client includes
+  tested X25519 + ML-KEM-768 primitives for sealing private mirror archives to
+  individual recipients; the end-to-end private-mirror workflow is not yet
+  wired into repository sync.
+- Release artifacts are content addressed and sha256 verified. Packaging exists
+  for AppImage, NSIS, and macOS disk images, with signing enabled when release
+  credentials are available.
+
+### Collaboration
+
+- Ed25519 node identities sign repository metadata and collaboration events.
+- Issues support status, priority, assignees, labels, milestones, custom fields,
+  attachments, and signed event histories.
+- Pull requests are signed patch submissions with mergeability checks, inline
+  review comments, approvals, change requests, and AI-assisted review.
+- Repository discussions, commit comments, reactions, notifications, and
+  subscriptions are implemented across the desktop and web surfaces.
+- Repository Actions run approved `.forkmesh/*.yml` workflows on push, release,
+  or manual dispatch and preserve run logs.
+- Claude Code and Codex sessions can be started from issues, run in parallel,
+  resumed, observed live, and turned into pull requests. Agent controls are
+  available in the desktop client, website, and IDE extension.
+
+### Funding
+
+- Profiles and repositories can publish Solana donation and payout addresses.
+- The Worker implements per-issue bounty deposit addresses, confirmation,
+  idempotent payout after merge, and public payout accounting. Bounty escrow is
+  custodial; the security and operator implications are documented at
+  [forkmesh.com/docs#bounty-custody](https://forkmesh.com/docs#bounty-custody).
+
+### Network and clients
+
+- The Python Cloudflare Worker serves the public site, account APIs, repository
+  catalog, signed inboxes, Git routing, and Durable Object coordination.
+- Repository rooms encrypt messages client-side with AES-256-GCM. Passphrase
+  rooms provide participant-only confidentiality.
+- ActivityPub exposes followable user and repository actors, publishes supported
+  collaboration events, accepts remote replies, and can turn qualifying repo
+  mentions into issue submissions. See the
+  [ActivityPub design notes](docs/design/activitypub-federation.md).
+- A Qt 6 desktop node, Flutter mobile client, web dashboard, and VS Code
+  extension are maintained in this repository.
+- The MCP helper in `tools/forkmesh_mcp_server.py` exposes repository reads and
+  file-backed issue and pull-request operations to MCP-capable agents.
+- Self-hosted mainnodes are supported through configurable relay URLs and the
+  Worker deployment flow.
+
+## Current limitations
+
+- The public deployment still relies on one primary mainnode for its catalog and
+  routing. Self-hosted relays coordinate nodes and payouts, but repository
+  catalogs do not yet converge across independent relays.
+- A mainnode stores coordination state, not Git objects. Browse, clone, and push
+  require at least one suitable desktop host to be online.
+- Git push authorization is owner-only; delegated collaborator push is not yet
+  implemented.
+- Private-mirror encryption primitives are implemented and tested, but archive
+  sealing, distribution, and restoration are not yet connected to the normal
+  repository mirroring flow.
+- The default repository room key is shared by ForkMesh clients. It prevents the
+  relay from reading stored ciphertext, but it is not confidential between
+  ForkMesh users. Use a room passphrase for participant-only confidentiality.
+- ActivityPub release announcements are wired into the desktop client; merge
+  announcements and desktop rendering of federated comments remain follow-ups.
+- ForkMesh is pre-1.0. Only the latest release and `main` receive fixes, and
+  security-sensitive deployments should review [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md).
+
